    "lem-lobachevsky-fourier-expansion-and-pi-third-bound": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-lobachevsky-function",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "For real $\\theta$, define the Lobachevsky function $$ \\Lambda(\\theta)=-\\int_0^\\theta \\log|2\\sin u|\\,du, $$ where the integral is interpreted improperly at zeros of $\\sin u$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.1",
            "2.1",
            "3.1",
            "4.1",
            "5.1"
          ]
        }
      ],
      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "For $|w|<1$, integrating the uniformly convergent geometric series on compact subdisks gives $-\\log(1-w)=\\sum_{n\\ge1}w^n/n$. Taking real parts at $w=re^{2iu}$, where $0<r<1$, yields $-\\log|1-re^{2iu}|=\\sum_{n\\ge1}r^n\\cos(2nu)/n$. This series is uniformly absolutely convergent in $u$ for fixed $r$, so integrating from $0$ to $\\theta$ gives $-\\int_0^\\theta\\log|1-re^{2iu}|\\,du=\\frac12\\sum_{n\\ge1}r^n\\sin(2n\\theta)/n^2$.",
          "step": "1.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-2-1",
          "claim": "For $r\\ge1/2$, $|1-re^{2iu}|^2=(1-r)^2+4r\\sin^2u$, so $\\sqrt2|\\sin u|\\le |1-re^{2iu}|\\le2$. Therefore $|\\log|1-re^{2iu}||$ is bounded by a constant plus $|\\log|\\sin u||$, which is integrable on bounded intervals. Dominated convergence sends the integral in step 1.1 to $\\Lambda(\\theta)$ as $r\\uparrow1$; the series converges uniformly in $\\theta$ because $\\sum n^{-2}<\\infty$. This proves the Fourier expansion on $[0,\\pi]$ and continuity there.",
          "step": "2.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 1.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-3-1",
          "claim": "The defining integrand is even, so $\\Lambda$ is odd. Its derivative away from the integral's singularities is $-\\log|2\\sin\\theta|$, a $\\pi$-periodic function; the Fourier formula gives $\\Lambda(0)=\\Lambda(\\pi)=0$, so the integral of this derivative over each period is zero. Hence $\\Lambda(\\theta+\\pi)=\\Lambda(\\theta)$ for every real $\\theta$. Substitution $\\theta=\\pi/2$ in the Fourier series gives $\\Lambda(\\pi/2)=0$.",
          "step": "3.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-4-1",
          "claim": "The identity $|2\\sin(2u)|=|2\\sin u|\\,|2\\sin(u+\\pi/2)|$ and substitution $v=2u$ in the defining integral give $\\Lambda(2\\theta)=2\\Lambda(\\theta)+2\\Lambda(\\theta+\\pi/2)-2\\Lambda(\\pi/2)$. Step 3.1 gives the stated duplication identity.",
          "step": "4.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 3.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "step-5-1",
          "claim": "In the Fourier series at $\\theta=\\pi/3$, $\\sin(2n\\pi/3)$ cycles through $\\sqrt3/2,-\\sqrt3/2,0$, so grouping successive triples gives the stated positive series. For $a=3k+1$, $a^{-2}-(a+1)^{-2}=(2a+1)/(a^2(a+1)^2)\\le2/a^3\\le2/(27k^3)$ for $k\\ge1$. For $N\\ge1$, the tail after $k=0,\\ldots,N$ is at most $\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\frac2{27}\\sum_{k>N}k^{-3}\\le\\frac{\\sqrt3}{4}\\frac2{27}\\int_N^\\infty x^{-3}\\,dx=\\frac{\\sqrt3}{108N^2}\\le\\frac{\\sqrt3}{72N^2}$.",
          "step": "5.1",
          "inputs": [
            "F1",
            "step 2.1",
            "algebra"
          ]
        }
      ],
