      "derivations": [
        {
          "id": "step-1-1",
          "claim": "The positive shapes determine nondegenerate ideal tetrahedra. The face maps are hyperbolic isometries. Around each finite edge, the edge product $1$ makes the accumulated complex similarity the identity, and the $2\\pi$ angle sum removes a cone angle. Thus the tetrahedra glue to a smooth hyperbolic metric away from the ideal vertex. Horospherical truncations give Euclidean triangles on the torus link; their face identifications are Euclidean similarities, maps of the form $x\\mapsto aRx+b$ with $a>0$, $R$ a rotation, and have no cone singularities. They define a Euclidean similarity structure: an atlas with these transition maps. Its linear holonomy, the product $aR$ accumulated around a loop, need not yet be trivial.",
