        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "def-knot-meridian-and-preferred-longitude",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice as in the chosen tubular-neighbourhood data. Let $K$ be a smooth oriented knot with chosen exterior $E_K$ and boundary torus $T_K=\\partial E_K$, and let $p:T_K\\to K$ be the projection of the chosen normal-disk tube. Orient each normal disk so that its positive tangent direction followed by its oriented normal basis agrees with the orientation of $S^3$. A **meridian** $\\mu$ is the oriented boundary of one such normal disk. A **degree-one parallel curve** is a simple closed curve $\\lambda$ in $T_K$ for which $p|_\\lambda:\\lambda\\to K$ has degree $+1$. A **preferred longitude** is a degree-one parallel curve satisfying $i_*[\\lambda]=0$ in $H_1(E_K;\\mathbb Z)$, where $i:T_K\\hookrightarrow E_K$ is inclusion. This is a condition on a curve; this definition alone asserts neither existence nor uniqueness. Reversing the orientation of $K$ reverses the orientations of both $\\mu$ and any degree-one parallel curve. If a subgroup $P\\le\\pi_1(E_K,x)$ is specified using a path from $x$ to $T_K$, record it only up to replacing it by $gPg^{-1}$ for $g\\in\\pi_1(E_K,x)$.",
          "uses": [
            "2.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-knot-exterior-has-peripheral-torus",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Assume countable choice. For a smooth oriented knot with a chosen product tubular neighbourhood $N(K)\\cong S^1\\times D^2$, let $\\mu$ be the positive fibre meridian and let $\\sigma$ be the product section $s\\mapsto(s,1)$ of $T_K=\\partial N(K)\\cong S^1\\times S^1$. Then $T_K=\\partial E_K$ is a torus and $[\\mu],[\\sigma]$ form a basis of $H_1(T_K;\\mathbb Z)$. The curve $\\sigma$ is a product section; this statement does not assert that it is the preferred zero-homology longitude.",
