    "lem-figure-eight-ideal-gluing-and-cusp-completeness": {
      "citations": [
        {
          "fact": "F1",
          "source": "def-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-and-cusp-holonomy",
          "source_section": "Definition",
          "quote": "Use the upper half-space model $$ \\mathbb H^3=\\{(x+iy,t):x,y\\in\\mathbb R,\\ t>0\\},\\qquad ds^2=t^{-2}(dx^2+dy^2+dt^2), $$ whose ideal boundary is $\\mathbb C\\cup\\{\\infty\\}$. An oriented ideal tetrahedron in normalized position has ideal vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$, where $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$. Its three edge-shape parameters are $$ z,\\qquad z'=\\frac1{1-z},\\qquad z''=\\frac{z-1}{z}, $$ each assigned to one pair of opposite edges; the parameter at a corner of a truncated ideal vertex triangle is the parameter of the incident edge. For an oriented path $\\gamma$ in a triangulated torus link of ideal vertices, its cusp multiplier $H(\\gamma)$ is the product of the corner parameters encountered along $\\gamma$, using the parameter when the path cuts off that corner on its left and its reciprocal when it cuts off the corner on its right.",
          "uses": [
            "3.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F2",
          "source": "lem-ideal-tetrahedron-shape-parameter-relations",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "For an oriented ideal tetrahedron with normalized vertices $0,1,\\infty,z$ and $\\operatorname{Im}z>0$, its three edge-shape parameters are $z$, $z'=1/(1-z)$, and $z''=(z-1)/z$. They satisfy $zz'z''=-1$, and their arguments in $(0,\\pi)$ are the three dihedral angles, whose sum is $\\pi$. For $\\omega=e^{i\\pi/3}$, the choice $z=\\omega$ gives $z=z'=z''=\\omega$ and all six dihedral angles equal to $\\pi/3$.",
          "uses": [
            "1.2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F3",
          "source": "lem-ideal-torus-cusp-holonomy-criterion",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let an orientable finite ideal triangulation have exactly one ideal vertex, whose link is a torus. Suppose its tetrahedra carry positive shapes whose edge products equal $1$ and whose dihedral angles sum to $2\\pi$ around every edge. If the cusp multipliers $H(\\alpha)$ and $H(\\beta)$ are $1$ for a basis $\\alpha,\\beta$ of the torus fundamental group, then the glued hyperbolic metric is complete at that cusp.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
        },
        {
          "fact": "F4",
          "source": "lem-horocusp-truncation-has-finite-volume",
          "source_section": "Statement",
          "quote": "Let $\\Gamma\\subset\\mathbb C$ be a rank-two lattice acting on $\\mathbb H^3$ by horizontal translations, and let $$ C=\\{(x+iy,t):t\\ge1\\}/\\Gamma $$ be the quotient of the horoball $t\\ge1$. If $A$ is the Euclidean area of a fundamental parallelogram for $\\Gamma$, then the end is at infinite hyperbolic distance and $\\operatorname{Vol}(C)=A/2$.",
          "uses": [
            "4.1"
          ]
